1
00:00:04,515 --> 00:00:05,390
Hello.

2
00:00:05,391 --> 00:00:14,510
This time, based on what we learned in Chapters 3, parts 1 and 2, let's look at the methods and principles of applying light to a picture.

3
00:00:14,511 --> 00:00:18,270
First, to help with understanding, I've made some spheres in 3D.

4
00:00:18,271 --> 00:00:21,710
There's a version without reflected light and a version with it.

5
00:00:21,711 --> 00:00:30,430
The two light sources come from the upper left, and I've placed the reflected light on the lower right.

6
00:00:30,431 --> 00:00:34,390
These spheres will be the guide for the light.

7
00:00:34,585 --> 00:00:44,210
According to what we learned last time, the position of the highlight etc. changes depending on the position of the sphere, and the position of the light source or the observer.

8
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:47,771
For this session, I'll set it so they don't change, for convenience.

9
00:00:48,105 --> 00:01:00,491
To add a bit more explanation, things like the highlight position changing with the light source position are better applied when drawing a background with large changes in the phase, rather than a character with relatively small changes.

10
00:01:03,535 --> 00:01:07,400
For the demonstration, I've brought a simple character key art sketch.

11
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:10,200
I'll place the guide sphere off to the side.

12
00:01:10,201 --> 00:01:12,680
First, I've used it as a sphere with no reflected light.

13
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:20,600
For the character, I've used the materials we use a lot: skin, cloth, leather, and metal, appropriately.

14
00:01:20,601 --> 00:01:24,440
Let me color it using what we've learned.

15
00:01:24,441 --> 00:01:31,400
First, let me lay down the base color like this and convert it to grayscale.

16
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:34,920
I'll determine the inherent value for each object and start.

17
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:40,920
They get brighter in the order of skin, metal, leather, then cloth.

18
00:01:40,921 --> 00:01:45,560
As coloring progresses, I have to not break this inherent value.

19
00:01:45,561 --> 00:01:47,720
Let's color the skin.

20
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:51,000
I'll work in grayscale to match the color of the sphere.

21
00:01:51,001 --> 00:01:59,480
Usually I don't use the glazing method at all because I don't feel its merit, but to help understand light, I'll proceed in a way similar to the glazing light approach.

22
00:02:00,044 --> 00:02:04,520
I'll let you know upfront that coloring this way in practice is inefficient.

23
00:02:05,619 --> 00:02:10,759
I'll look at the angle and light amount of each face of this sphere, which has no reflected light, and apply it to the skin.

24
00:02:12,015 --> 00:02:15,521
An understanding of the human form is essential.

25
00:02:16,410 --> 00:02:23,781
Grasp the faces of the human body broadly, substitute them into the sphere, measure the strength of the shading, and paint it.

26
00:02:24,570 --> 00:02:29,580
I won't consider the inherent value.

27
00:02:29,581 --> 00:02:31,740
It'd be skin of the same material as the sphere.

28
00:02:40,570 --> 00:02:48,220
Find where the faces of the human body and the faces of the sphere align, and it's easy to sample with the eyedropper and paint.

29
00:02:48,221 --> 00:02:49,820
Split the faces broadly, into big chunks.

30
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:55,171
Divide the areas like this and substitute them into the sphere.

31
00:02:55,505 --> 00:02:57,530
Then repeat the painting.

32
00:02:59,030 --> 00:03:02,801
In practice, this sphere needs to exist in your head.

33
00:03:03,634 --> 00:03:07,900
Not just a sphere, but a sphere of skin material.

34
00:03:07,901 --> 00:03:12,380
If that's the case, you won't need to use the glazing method like this.

35
00:03:12,381 --> 00:03:14,140
Because you can paint right away.

36
00:03:40,510 --> 00:03:44,800
For the face, split the faces too, recalling the facial anatomy we learned.

37
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:49,020
Let me put the shadow only on the neck.

38
00:04:22,010 --> 00:04:27,660
If you've split the faces broadly and painted, blend them naturally.

39
00:04:27,661 --> 00:04:29,740
Using the mixer brush tool makes it easy.

40
00:04:41,525 --> 00:04:44,670
There are several ways to blend.

41
00:04:44,671 --> 00:04:53,710
There's the method of weakening the pen pressure and extracting with the eyedropper while mixing, and the method of mixing with the mixer brush tool like this.

42
00:04:53,711 --> 00:04:55,710
Just do whichever way is comfortable for you.

43
00:04:57,555 --> 00:05:02,700
When blending like this, you have to not break the faces we've split too much.

44
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:50,220
Usually, when doing glazing, you paint like this and cover the color using an overlay layer etc.

45
00:05:50,221 --> 00:05:57,660
But without considering the inherent value I mentioned earlier and last time, it will become a bad picture.

46
00:05:57,661 --> 00:06:01,701
For comparison, I've duplicated layers and left them.

47
00:06:02,115 --> 00:06:05,980
This new layer will be corrected to match the skin's inherent value.

48
00:06:06,570 --> 00:06:10,620
Skin isn't as high-contrast as you'd think.

49
00:06:10,621 --> 00:06:13,500
Press Control and M to open the curve window and adjust it.

50
00:06:13,890 --> 00:06:21,220
If you set from bright to dark as one, two, three, four, and five, raise the middle part, three.

51
00:06:22,615 --> 00:06:27,680
And lower two and four a bit so the midtones widen.

52
00:06:29,230 --> 00:06:39,310
I raised five and lowered one a bit so the dark doesn't become too dark and the highlight doesn't pop too much.

53
00:06:41,555 --> 00:06:45,340
It now has an inherent value similar to the skin tone.

54
00:06:45,341 --> 00:06:49,861
Let me overlay the skin color on top of this.

55
00:06:57,820 --> 00:07:01,750
Change the layer property to overlay and check it.

56
00:07:02,625 --> 00:07:04,460
The skin first pass is done.

57
00:07:04,461 --> 00:07:05,900
Not hard, right?

58
00:07:05,901 --> 00:07:08,620
Let's also apply the reflected light.

59
00:07:08,621 --> 00:07:15,781
Let me go back to grayscale and swap the sphere with reflected light in, and paint while setting it.

60
00:07:47,315 --> 00:07:50,300
You shouldn't paint the parts where the reflected light is blocked.

61
00:07:50,301 --> 00:07:51,500
It's these parts.

62
00:07:51,501 --> 00:07:54,220
The body blocks it, so the reflected light can't reach.

63
00:07:54,221 --> 00:07:57,260
The reflected light reflecting off the body can form.

64
00:07:57,261 --> 00:08:04,940
But for now, I'll only consider the reflected light coming from the reflected light source, not the reflected light scattered from the skin.

65
00:08:04,941 --> 00:08:08,300
If you turn on the overlay layer, it becomes skin with the reflected light applied.

66
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:18,080
To go into deeper coloring, you'd have to consider the color of this reflected light, the shift in the skin's hue, subsurface scattering, and so on.

67
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:21,641
I'll cover deeper coloring in a later chapter.

68
00:08:23,095 --> 00:08:27,040
You can draw other parts with the same principle.

69
00:08:27,041 --> 00:08:33,200
For cloth, it has even less contrast than skin, so the highlight forms less.

70
00:08:33,201 --> 00:08:40,880
So even though it's drawn as the same material as the sphere, substituted into the sphere, you absolutely have to reduce the contrast through correction.

71
00:09:49,540 --> 00:09:53,690
I lowered the bright parts considerably with curve adjustment.

72
00:09:53,691 --> 00:09:54,810
And raised the dark parts a lot too.

73
00:10:02,305 --> 00:10:05,860
Likewise, raise an overlay layer to add color.

74
00:10:13,615 --> 00:10:18,360
For leather, it's good to have just a little bit more contrast than skin.

75
00:10:54,339 --> 00:10:55,340
Do the same curve correction on it.

76
00:11:02,410 --> 00:11:04,300
Raise an overlay layer.

77
00:11:15,655 --> 00:11:20,201
It looked too high in contrast, so I adjusted it again.

78
00:11:47,639 --> 00:11:48,640
Metal has the strongest contrast.

79
00:11:48,641 --> 00:11:51,961
I'm drawing without considering reflected objects.

80
00:12:18,250 --> 00:12:23,080
Along with the value contrast of the material, I draw with the inherent value in mind too.

81
00:12:50,445 --> 00:12:58,700
Now that I know this value application principle, instead of glazing, let me color again while controlling hue and value together.

82
00:12:58,701 --> 00:13:03,260
Naturally, it's important to draw while recalling the inherent value for each material.

83
00:13:04,840 --> 00:13:07,560
I enjoy this task the most.

84
00:13:07,561 --> 00:13:16,760
Compared to the total work time, it takes less than five percent of the time, but I feel like almost fifty percent of the completion gets packed in.

85
00:13:16,761 --> 00:13:21,080
The further into the later stages you go, the less the picture changes relative to the time you put in.

86
00:13:21,081 --> 00:13:23,480
It's tiring and hard work.

87
00:13:23,481 --> 00:13:28,840
Within a month, my classmates will be working this happily too.

88
00:13:28,841 --> 00:13:29,400
Enjoy it.

89
00:13:29,401 --> 00:13:30,520
Because the hard part is ahead.

90
00:13:32,090 --> 00:13:36,060
I told you earlier that this first-pass coloring is the most important.

91
00:13:36,061 --> 00:13:38,220
It determines the completion.

92
00:13:38,221 --> 00:13:43,340
The client's confirmation of the coloring draft also happens in this process.

93
00:13:44,515 --> 00:13:49,660
Should I say it's decided here whether the picture becomes a golden spoon or a black spoon?

94
00:13:49,661 --> 00:13:59,900
Even if it becomes a black spoon in this process, a good picture can still come out, but the reality is that a golden spoon is easy to succeed at, while a black spoon requires blood, sweat, and tears.

95
00:13:59,901 --> 00:14:00,901
Because of that, when I.

96
00:14:00,990 --> 00:14:08,240
If I don't like the picture, I go back to this stage, redo the light setup, and enter the first-pass coloring.

97
00:14:08,241 --> 00:14:10,480
To be reborn as a golden spoon.

98
00:14:10,481 --> 00:14:13,881
In reality it's impossible, but in drawing it's possible.

99
00:15:12,410 --> 00:15:17,420
On the premise that the sketch and base color are laid down, this first pass takes ten minutes.

100
00:15:17,795 --> 00:15:19,500
It's a low-pressure amount of time.

101
00:15:19,501 --> 00:15:21,740
Because of that, you can make various attempts.

102
00:15:22,250 --> 00:15:25,500
The mood of a picture changes a lot depending on the light.

103
00:15:25,501 --> 00:15:28,821
Let me do a first-pass coloring using the light spheres.

104
00:15:31,870 --> 00:15:37,440
For metal, as we learned last time, work thinking there's something around it.

105
00:15:37,441 --> 00:15:43,040
Be sure to put the light source and observer, and it'd be good to set just one or two more things.

106
00:15:43,150 --> 00:15:46,441
It'd be great to consider the character's body or clothes too.

107
00:15:50,620 --> 00:15:54,510
This accessory on the forearm is cylindrical in shape.

108
00:15:54,511 --> 00:16:00,270
Since we learned how to paint cylindrical metal last time, you can draw it without much difficulty.

109
00:16:00,271 --> 00:16:04,990
It happens to be tilted to match the position of the light source, so the highlight is very visible.

110
00:16:08,250 --> 00:16:16,250
I'll paint the highlight.

111
00:16:16,251 --> 00:16:19,130
Then I'll paint the reflected color of the clothes here.

112
00:16:26,250 --> 00:16:29,130
And I'll paint the observer too.

113
00:16:29,131 --> 00:16:30,131
It'd be facing front, right?

114
00:16:35,870 --> 00:16:39,440
And since the background and floor are a light gray.

115
00:16:40,790 --> 00:16:44,561
I'll mix it with the metal's own slight orange tint and paint a bright color.

116
00:16:46,270 --> 00:16:52,121
If you sample the background with the eyedropper and adjust the pen pressure and paint, it'll mix into a natural color.

117
00:17:03,870 --> 00:17:08,190
Shall we look at the other side too?

118
00:17:08,191 --> 00:17:12,471
Here it's largely blocked by clothes, so I'll paint it dark.

119
00:17:20,669 --> 00:17:24,840
And this bracelet on the other side is cylindrical too.

120
00:17:30,464 --> 00:17:33,660
For the right part here, I'll paint the reflected light.

121
00:17:35,874 --> 00:17:42,350
For this middle part, it faces the ground, and judging by the angle, it's quite far from the ground.

122
00:17:42,351 --> 00:17:44,990
The farther away, the weaker the light.

123
00:17:44,991 --> 00:17:46,910
I'll paint the reflected light lightly.

124
00:18:11,324 --> 00:18:14,600
And this left part is projected from the skin.

125
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:23,020
I'll apply that color.

126
00:18:23,021 --> 00:18:26,901
The rest of the metal can be drawn through this kind of calculation.

127
00:19:04,479 --> 00:19:06,000
What about hair?

128
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,040
The head, broadly speaking, is a sphere shape, so why does a ring-shaped highlight form?

129
00:19:11,041 --> 00:19:12,960
I've drawn a head to explain.

130
00:19:14,749 --> 00:19:18,560
I think many of you have drawn this ring-shaped highlight.

131
00:19:20,469 --> 00:19:22,920
Let me approach it theoretically.

132
00:19:22,921 --> 00:19:25,640
A person's head isn't just a sphere shape.

133
00:19:25,641 --> 00:19:29,720
It's countless strands of hair gathered together to form a sphere shape.

134
00:19:31,024 --> 00:19:34,451
To help understanding, I made a model in 3D again.

135
00:19:34,894 --> 00:19:40,010
I made it assuming the hair is in eight lumps.

136
00:19:40,011 --> 00:19:42,490
Where do the highlights form?

137
00:19:42,491 --> 00:19:43,850
They form in two places here.

138
00:19:45,495 --> 00:19:48,720
Because the surface is bumpy.

139
00:19:48,721 --> 00:19:53,200
What happens if the hair is spread to sixteen lumps?

140
00:19:53,201 --> 00:19:54,880
There are more highlights now.

141
00:19:57,124 --> 00:19:59,360
Shall we look at thirty-two lumps?

142
00:19:59,361 --> 00:20:00,361
There are more, right?

143
00:20:01,959 --> 00:20:05,080
What happens if there are countless strands of hair?

144
00:20:05,081 --> 00:20:06,200
It becomes a ring shape.

145
00:20:07,429 --> 00:20:11,400
That's why a ring-shaped highlight forms on a person's hair.

146
00:20:12,569 --> 00:20:23,340
In this way, if you have a high understanding of the surface material of an object and of light, you can draw a realistic picture well without even referencing a photo.

147
00:20:23,340 --> 00:20:29,180
The more you know about light, the harder it gets, but I think you don't have to be too afraid of it.

148
00:20:29,181 --> 00:20:32,821
It's good to be accurate, but it doesn't have to be perfect.

149
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:35,180
As long as it's not clumsy.

150
00:20:35,181 --> 00:20:38,380
Because drawing a good-looking picture comes first.

151
00:20:38,381 --> 00:20:42,460
Even with a moderate understanding like mine, you can make a living drawing pictures.

152
00:20:43,294 --> 00:20:49,560
My only wish is that the light in the pictures of my classmates who take this class won't be clumsy.

153
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:55,150
In this chapter, we learned about light and color.

154
00:20:55,150 --> 00:21:00,350
From the next chapter, we'll begin character concept art in earnest.

155
00:21:00,351 --> 00:21:01,351
Great work.
